          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            A
            %em derived functor
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            induced from, “derived from” or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} presented by
            an ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
          %p
            Historically the concept first arose in the special context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+chain+complexes"} categories of chain complexes
            and is often still understood by default in this special sense. The relation to the general case is discussed below in the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#InHomologicalAlgebra"} In homological algebra
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor+in+homological+algebra"} derived functor in homological algebra
          %h3#general_idea General idea
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            serves as a presentation for an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
            by
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} simplicial localization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to D
            should induce an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            between the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C} \to \mathbf{D}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            = succeed "." do
              %strong derived functor
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+functor"} homotopical functor
            in that it respects the weak equivalences in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} simplicial localization
            it extends to a functor of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-categories
            and this is the corresponding
            %em derived functor
            \.
          %p
            However, typically functors of interest do not respect weak equivalences and hence do not uniquely or even naturally give rise to an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y \in C
            that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} equivalent
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
            but not
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphic
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(x)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(y)
            that are equivalent in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{D}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-functor
            would. So it matters on which representatives of a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+class"} equivalence class
            of objects the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is applied.
          %p
            Remembering that by Dwyer-Kan
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+localization"} simplicial localization
            the morphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{D}
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zig-zags"} zig-zags
            of morphisms in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to be a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝔻
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{D}F : \mathbf{C} \to \mathbf{D}
            induced from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal property
            of the localization by a functor of the form
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi Q
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \circ Q : C \to D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q : C \to C
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/endofunctor"} endofunctor
            which is naturally connected to the identity by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zig-zag"} zig-zag
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 2
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mover
                    %mo ←
                    %mo ≃
                  %mi Q
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow}
                  X_1
                  \\stackrel{\simeq}{\to}
                  X_2
                  \\cdots
                  \\stackrel{\simeq}{\leftarrow}
                  Q X
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here if this zig-zag consists just of one morphism to the left one would speak of a
            = succeed "." do
              %strong left derived functor
            = succeed "." do
              %strong right derived functor
          %h3#on_model_categories On model categories
          %p
            In highly structured situations where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are equipped not just with weak equivalences but with the full structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            and if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a left or right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} Quillen functor
            with respect to these model structures, there are accordingly more structured ways to solve this problem:
          %p
            The
            %strong left derived functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕃
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{L}F : \mathbf{C} \to \mathbf{D}
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+Quillen+functor"} left Quillen functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to D
            is obtained by applying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cofibrant+objects"} cofibrant objects
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %strong right derived functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}G : \mathbf{D} \to \mathbf{C}
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+Quillen+functor"} right Quillen functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G : D \to C
            is obtained by applying
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibrant+objects"} fibrant objects
          %p
            Recalling that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presentable+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} presented
            by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            may be identified with the full
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mo ∘
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\circ
            of fibrant-cofibrant objects, this may be understood as ensuring that the derived functor indeed respects the
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ←
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (F \dashv G) : C \stackrel{\leftarrow}{\to} D
          %p
            an
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} Quillen adjunction
            between
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+categories"} simplicial model categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            with cofibrant-fibrant replacement induces a pair of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} adjoint (∞,1)-functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕃
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ⟵
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi D
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi ℝ
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{L}F
                  \\colon
                  \\mathbf{C} \stackrel{\longleftarrow}{\longrightarrow} \mathbf{D}
                  \\colon
                  \\mathbb{R}G
          %p
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-category"} quasi-categories
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C} = N(C^\circ)
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                    %mo =
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D = N(D^\circ)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+coherent+nerve"} homotopy coherent nerve
            functor.
          %p
            Often a simplified version of this situation is considered, where instead of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{C}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{D}
            only
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+an+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} their homotopy categories
            are remembered, equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+a+model+category"} homotopy categories of the model categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functors"} adjoint (∞,1)-functors
            restrict to functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ⟵
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi R
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  L F \colon
                  Ho(C) \stackrel{\longleftarrow}{\longrightarrow} Ho(D)
                  \\colon
                  R G
          %p
            on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+a+model+category"} homotopy categories
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+derived+functor"} total
            %strong derived functors
            in the literature. For more on this see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category+of+a+model+category"} homotopy category of a model category
            the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "homotopy+category+of+a+model+category#DerivedFunctors"} derived functors
          %p
            More generally, derived functors in this sense may be considered in situations where less than the above extra structure is available (no
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            structure or not
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} Quillen adjunction
          %h3#on_homotopy_categories On homotopy categories
          %p
            If one forgets the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nPOV"} nPOV
            and that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            should be regarded as presentation for an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+functor"} homotopical functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon C \longrightarrow D
            is to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extension"} extend
            it to a diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi F
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi Q
                                  %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo ?
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mpadded{:width => "0"}
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi Q
                                  %mi D
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ho
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi Ho
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  C &amp;\stackrel{F}{\to}&amp; D
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^{\mathrlap{Q_C}} &amp;(?)&amp; \downarrow^{\mathrlap{Q_D}}
                  \\\
                  Ho(C) &amp;\to&amp; Ho(D)
                  }
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi C
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi C
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_C \colon C \longrightarrow Ho(C)
            is the universal morphism characterizing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
            and similarly for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_D
          %p
            There is a general method of ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+theory"} category theory
            to solve such problems universally: one may take
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Ho
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi D
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho(C) \to Ho(D)
            to be either the left or right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Q
                    %mi d
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_d \circ F
            along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Q
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q_C
          %p
            In the literature this is often taken as the
            %em definition
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+derived+functor"} total
            left or right derived functors. Unfortunately, it is not clear how this definition by Kan extension relates to what should be the right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category+theory"} (∞,1)-category theoretic
            situation above. Moreover, the examples of derived functors that play a role in practice are effectively always constructed instead rather by combining
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            with cofibrant/fibrant or similar replacement functors. It is then but a happy byproduct that the functors so obtained also happen to be left or right Kan extensions.
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            We first give the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/decategorification"} decategorified
            definition of total derived functors on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy categories
            in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#OnHomotopyCat"} As functors on homotopy categories
          %p
            and then the
            = succeed "-version" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-category"} (∞,1)-category
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#OnInftyCats"}> As functors on (∞,1)-categories
              \.
          %p
            The special case of derived functors in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            is discussed from this general perspective in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#InHomologicalAlgebra"}> In homological algebra
              \.
          %p
            A dedicated discussion of this case is at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors+in+homological+algebra"} derived functors in homological algebra
          %h3#OnHomotopyCat As functors on homotopy categories
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Core
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi W
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Core(C) \hookrightarrow W \hookrightarrow C
            a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : C \to D
            any functor, the
            %strong left derived functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi L
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L F
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is the
            %em right
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            along the projection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Ho
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p : C \to Ho_C
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi F
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi p
                        %mtd
                          %mo ⇑
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mo ↗
                            %mrow
                              %mi L
                              %mi F
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi Ho
                            %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  C &amp;&amp;\stackrel{F}{\to}&amp;&amp; D
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow^p &amp;\Uparrow&amp; \nearrow_{L F}
                  \\\
                  Ho_C
                  }
          %p
            (if it exists). Dually, the
            %strong right derived functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R F
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is its
            %em left
            Kan extension along
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p
            More generally, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            is itself a category with weak equivalences, then by derived functors of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            we often mean derived functors of the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %mi D
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Ho
                    %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \stackrel{F}{\to} D \to Ho_D
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              By the universal property of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Ho
                        %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho_C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Ho
                      %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ho_C \to D
              are equivalent to functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C\to D
              which take weak equivalences to isomorphisms. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              itself takes weak equivalences to isomorphisms, then its left and right derived functors are both (isomorphic to) its unique extension along
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi L
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L F
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R F
              are not extensions of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              even up to isomorphism.
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p
              In practice, derived functors are usually computed using fibrant and cofibrant
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
              replacements (see the entries on
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
              and
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+retract+of+a+homotopical+category"} deformation retracts
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_3 Remark
            %p
              If the codomain admits sufficiently many
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
              can be computed in terms of those, and that such Kan extensions are called
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "Kan%20extension#Pointwise"} pointwise
              %em construction
              of a derived functor is not possible.
            %p
              However, when derived functors are constructed using fibrant and cofibrant replacements, as above, it turns out
              %em a posteriori
              that they are actually pointwise: they are preserved by all representable functors, and hence their individual object values have the universal property of the (generally large) limits that would have been used to compute them, even though not all limits exist in the homotopy category. In fact, derived functors constructed in this way are actually
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolute+colimit"} absolute
              Kan extensions: preserved by any functor whatsoever.
          %h3#OnInftyCats
            As functors on
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
              by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+model+category"} simplicial model categories
              and let
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mi F
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mi G
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (F \dashv G) : C \stackrel{\overset{G}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{F}{\to}} D
            %p
              be an
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+functor"} enriched
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} Quillen adjunction
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} (∞,1)-adjunction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi 𝕃
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi 𝕃
                          %mi F
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℝ
                          %mi G
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\mathbb{L}F \dashv \mathbb{R}G) :
                    N(C^\circ)
                    \\stackrel{\overset{\mathbb{R} G}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{\mathbb{L}F}{\to}}
                    N(D^\circ)
            %p
              between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-categories"} quasi-categories
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(C^\circ)
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi D
                      %mo ∘
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(D^\circ)
              otained as the
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+coherent+nerve"} homotopy coherent nerve
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sSet"} sSet
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategories"} subcategories
              of fibrant-cofibrant objects. Their image on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy categories
              produces the notion of total derived functor between homotopy categories discussed above
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi L
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi R
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %mi L
                          %mi F
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mrow
                          %mi R
                          %mi G
                    %mi Ho
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi D
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (L F \dashv R G) :
                    Ho(C)
                    \\stackrel{\overset{R G}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{L F}{\to}}
                    Ho(D)
                    \\,.
          %p
            This is prop. 5.2.4.6 and remark 5.2.4.7 in (
            %a{:href => "#Lurie"}> Lurie
            ). For more along these lines see also at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Quillen+adjunction#AssociatedInfinityAdjunction"} Quillen adjunction - Associated infinity-adjunction
          %h3#InHomologicalAlgebra In homological algebra
          %p
            Often and traditionally, the concept of derived functors is considered in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            exclusively in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+chain+complexes"} categories of chain complexes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{A})
            in an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
          %ul
            %li
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functor+in+homological+algebra"} derived functor in homological algebra
          %p Here put that special case a bit more into the general perspective.
          %p
            By taking
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{A})
            is naturally a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} category with weak equivalences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} model structure on chain complexes
            is implicit. For instance, an injective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resolution"} resolution
            of chain complexes is nothing but a fibrant replacement in the injective model structure. Dually, a projective resolution is a cofibrant replacement in the projective model structure. (Note, though, that hypotheses on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            are required in order for these model structures to exist.)
          %p
            Now, any ordinary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/additive+functor"} additive functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F\colon \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{B}
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+categories"} abelian categories
            induces a functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_\bullet(F)\colon Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{A}) \to Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{B})
            between
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} categories of chain complexes
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_\bullet(F)
          %p
            Note first that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_\bullet(F)
            automatically preserves
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homotopies"} chain homotopies
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+homotopy+equivalence"} chain homotopy equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_\bullet(F)
            automatically preserves weak equivalences between projective-cofibrant objects, and also between injective-fibrant objects. Thus, it has a left derived functor if the projective model structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{A})
            exists, and a right derived functor if the injective model structure exists.
          %p
            In the homological algebra literature, what is called the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi p
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo →
                  %mi ℬ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  R^p F \colon \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{B}
          %p is the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mover
                    %mo ↪
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mi B
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒜
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %msub
                        %mi Ch
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi H
                        %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ℬ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{A} \stackrel{\mathbf{B}^p (-)}{\hookrightarrow} Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{A})
                  \\stackrel{\mathbb{R} Ch_\bullet(F)}{\to}
                  Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{B})
                  \\stackrel{H^0(-)}{\to}
                  \\mathcal{B}
                  \\,,
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The first map sends an object
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒜
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \in \mathcal{A}
                to the corresponding
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+object"} Eilenberg-MacLane object
                = succeed ":" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi B
                          %mi p
                        %mi A
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{B}^p A
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A[p]
                concentrated on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
                in degree
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            %li
              %p
                The second map is the actual right derived functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %msub
                        %mi Ch
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}Ch_\bullet(F)
                of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Ch
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_\bullet(F)
                in the sense used previously on this page. Thus, this is itself the composite
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi F
                      %mo :
                      %msub
                        %mi Ch
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi P
                      %msub
                        %mi Ch
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi 𝒜
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                          %msub
                            %mi Ch
                            %mo •
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %msub
                        %mi Ch
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℬ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %mo ,
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                      \\mathbb{R}F : Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{A}) \stackrel{P}{\to}
                      Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{A})
                      \\stackrel{Ch_\bullet(F)}{\to}
                      Ch_\bullet(\mathcal{B})
                      \\,,
              %p
                where
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                denotes a fibrant resolution functor in the injective
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} model structure on chain complexes
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+resolutions"} injective resolutions
                seen in the homological algebra literature.
            %li
              %p
                The last morphism computes the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochain+cohomology"} cochain cohomology
                of the resulting cochain complex in degree 0.
          %p
            Of course, it is equivalent to instead regard
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            as concentrated in degree
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
          %p The first and the last steps are traditionally included, but are not really necessary:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                Instead of applying the first step and restricting attention to arguments that are chain complexes concentrated in a single degree, one can evaluate
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℝ
                      %msub
                        %mi Ch
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R} Ch_\bullet(F)
                on all chain complexes (and then, if desired, take homology groups). In homological algebra one then speaks of
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyper-derived+functors"} hyper-derived functors
            %li
              %p
                The last step of taking cohomology groups serves to extract invariant and computable information. It also destroys the simple composition law of functors, though. But there is a computational tool that can be used to recover the derived functor – in this homological sense – of the composite of two functors from their individual derivations: this is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectral+sequence"} spectral sequence
                called the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/spectral+sequence#GrothendieckSpectralSequence"} Grothendieck spectral sequence
          %h4#long_exact_sequences Long exact sequences
          %p
            Traditionally, in homological algebra, one only takes left derived functors of right exact functors, and right derived functors of left exact ones. As we saw above, both left and right derived functors can be
            %em defined
            without these hypotheses, but it is only in the presence of these hypotheses that we obtain
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/long+exact+sequences"} long exact sequences
          %p
            Specifically, suppose we have a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/short+exact+sequence"} short exact sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to A \to B \to C \to 0
          %p
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒜
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{A}
            has
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enough+projectives"} enough projectives
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+resolutions"} projective resolutions
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Q
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q B
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q C
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to Q A \to Q B \to Q C \to 0
          %p
            is a short exact sequence of chain complexes. But since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q C
            is projective, this short exact sequence is split, and therefore preserved by any additive functor. Thus we have another short exact sequence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0 \to F Q A \to F Q B \to F Q C \to 0
          %p which therefore gives rise to a long exact sequence in homology:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \to H_1(F Q A) \to H_1(F Q B) \to H_1(F Q C) \to H_0 (F Q A) \to H_0(F Q B) \to H_0(F Q C).
          %p
            Of course, these homology groups are precisely the left derived functors of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %p
            All of this works without hypothesis on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+exact+functor"} right exact
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Q
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 1
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mn 0
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Q A_1 \to Q A_0 \to A \to 0
          %p
            (and the analogous ones for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            and
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mi A
                  %mo ≅
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F A \cong H_0 (F Q A)
            and so on, so that the above long exact sequence actually finishes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi Q
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi F
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mn 0
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdots \to H_1(F Q A) \to H_1(F Q B) \to H_1(F Q C) \to F A \to F B \to F C \to 0.
          %p This is how derived functors are traditionally introduced in homological algebra: as a way to continue the right half of a short exact sequence preserved by a right exact functor into a long exact sequence. The case of left exact functors and right derived functors is dual.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The (total) derived functor of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limit"} limit
                functor is the
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"} homotopy limit
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi lim
                        %mrow
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi i
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} lim^{(i)}
                often called the derived functors of Lim are then given by the (co)homology of that ‘total’ form.
            %li
              %p
                More generally, the total derived functor of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+extension"} Kan extension
                is
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+extension"} homotopy Kan extension
            %li
              %p
                In the context of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} model structure on chain complexes
                of
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
                functor and the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-functor"} hom-functor
                are called
                = succeed "-functor" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tor"} Tor
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ext-functor"} Ext-functor
            %li
              %p
                A
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+direct+image"} derived direct image
                functor computes
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+inverse+image"} derived inverse image
          %h2#functoriality Functoriality
          %p
            Passage to left derived functors is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudofunctor"} pseudofunctor
            from a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-category"} 2-category
            of model categories, left Quillen functors, and natural transformations to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+pseudofunctor"} double pseudofunctor
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category"} double category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+category+of+model+categories"} of model categories
            to the double category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quintets"} quintets
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mates"} mates
            are also preserved by deriving, even when they relate composites of left and right Quillen functors; see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Shulman"} (Shulman)
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hyper-derived+functor"}> hyper-derived functor
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/total+derived+functor"} total derived functor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delta-functor"} delta-functor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-functor"} adjoint (∞,1)-functor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+adjunction+unit"} derived adjunction unit
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+limit"}> homotopy limit
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+colimit"} homotopy colimit
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+Kan+extension"} homotopy Kan extension
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/satellite"} satellite
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The notion of derived functors is due to:
          %ul
            %li#CartanEilenberg56
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Henri+Cartan"}> Henri Cartan
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Samuel+Eilenberg"} Samuel Eilenberg
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homological+Algebra"} Homological Algebra
              %strong 19
              (1999) &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://press.princeton.edu/books/paperback/9780691049915/homological-algebra-pms-19-volume-19"}> ISBN:9780691049915
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1515/9781400883844"} doi:10.1515/9781400883844
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.stonybrook.edu/~mmovshev/BOOKS/homologicalalgeb033541mbp.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            General discussion of derived functors in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
          %ul
            %li#Adams74
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Adams"}> Frank Adams
                , part III, section 8 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stable+homotopy+and+generalised+homology"} Stable homotopy and generalised homology
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Dwyer"}> William Dwyer
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Philip+Hirschhorn"} Philip Hirschhorn
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Kan"} Daniel Kan
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeff+Smith"} Jeff Smith
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Homotopy+Limit+Functors+on+Model+Categories+and+Homotopical+Categories"} Homotopy Limit Functors on Model Categories and Homotopical Categories
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruno+Kahn"}> Bruno Kahn
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Georges+Maltsiniotis"} Georges Maltsiniotis
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Structures+de+D%C3%A9rivabilit%C3%A9"} Structures de Dérivabilité
          %p
            and with an eye towards
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+sheaf+cohomology"} abelian sheaf cohomology
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ugo+Bruzzo"}> Ugo Bruzzo
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Derived functors and sheaf cohomology
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/11473"} doi:10.1142/11473
          %p
            Discussion in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28%E2%88%9E%2C1%29-categories"} (∞,1)-categories
            is in section 5.2.4 of
          %ul
            %li#Lurie
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Lurie"}> Jacob Lurie
              ,
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Higher+Topos+Theory"} Higher Topos Theory
          %p The double-categorical functoriality is in
          %ul
            %li#Shulman
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mike+Shulman"}> Mike Shulman
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Comparing composites of left and right derived functors
              %a{:href => "http://nyjm.albany.edu/j/2011/17-5.html"} NYJM
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"}> Emily Riehl
              , Chapter 2 in:
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categorical+Homotopy+Theory"} Categorical Homotopy Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781107261457"} doi:10.1017/CBO9781107261457
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.jhu.edu/~eriehl/cathtpy.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            A more modern definition with better theoretical properties and with emphasis on the role of correspondences in
            = succeed "-categorical" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vladimir+Hinich"}> Vladimir Hinich
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em So, what is a derived functor?
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1811.12255"} arXiv:1811:12255
          %h3#in_homological_algebra_2 In homological algebra
          %p
            A standard textbook introduction to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+functors+in+homological+algebra"} derived functors in homological algebra
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Charles+Weibel"}> Charles Weibel
              ,
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/An+Introduction+to+Homological+Algebra"} An Introduction to Homological Algebra
          %p
            A systematic discussion of this case from the point of view of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localization
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            is in section 13 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Masaki+Kashiwara"}> Masaki Kashiwara
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Schapira"} Pierre Schapira
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+and+Sheaves"} Categories and Sheaves
          %p and, similarly, in section 7 of
          %ul
            %li#Schapira
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pierre+Schapira"}> Pierre Schapira
              ,
              %em Categories and homological algebra
              (2011) (
              %a{:href => "http://people.math.jussieu.fr/~schapira/lectnotes/HomAl.pdf"}> pdf
              )
        .revisedby
